{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U11.4", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both their logic-side ground (GND1, pin 4) and bus-side ground (GND2, pin 5) connected to the same GND net.",
      "expected": "For an isolated CAN transceiver, GND1 should connect to the logic ground and GND2 should connect to a separate isolated CAN ground (e.g., GND_ISO) to maintain galvanic isolation.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation of the CAN buses is defeated. Ground loops and common-mode voltages can couple between the logic side and the CAN bus side, potentially damaging the transceivers or connected equipment and causing communication errors.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R36 has value \"24k\" but its description and MPN (FRC0805J240TSD) indicate a 24Ω resistor.",
      "expected": "R36 is a bleeder resistor across the PoE output (VOUTP/VOUTN). A 24kΩ value is appropriate; a 24Ω part would draw excessive current and dissipate excessive power.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the board is assembled using the MPN/description, a 24Ω resistor will be placed across the PoE output, causing a near-short, high power dissipation, and likely damage to the resistor or power source. The intended 24kΩ bleeder would be correct.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R10"],
      "pins": ["R10.1", "R10.2"],
      "nets": ["PI_n_LED_ACT", "Net-(D4-K)"],
      "observed": "R10 has value \"1M\" but its description and MPN (0603WAF1001T5E) indicate a 1kΩ resistor.",
      "expected": "R10 is a current-limiting resistor for the green activity LED D4. A 1kΩ value is typical; 1MΩ would make the LED extremely dim or invisible.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R10\") (value \"1M\") ... (description \" 100mW Thick Film Resistors 75V ±100ppm/℃ ±1% 1kΩ 0603 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \" 0603WAF1001T5E\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the value field is used for assembly, the LED will be nearly off. If the MPN is used, the LED will work correctly. The design file is inconsistent and could lead to a non-functional indicator.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain a serious anomaly: the isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have their logic and bus grounds tied together, defeating the intended galvanic isolation. Additionally, R36 and R10 have value fields that disagree with their MPN/description, which could lead to incorrect parts being installed."
}

